label Day7
\Outside the studio, visitors crowd the corridor with damp programmes. #id:line.Day7.one
\Inside the narrow warm-up room behind the stage, the crowd noise drops to a muffled hum through the double acoustic doors. #id:line.Day7.002
\Beside the dressing counter, Lavinia stands with her weight settled into her left hip. Her right heel stays half an inch clear of the floorboards—the exact guarded transfer from before she went away, when she insisted to the department that nothing was wrong. #id:line.Day7.003
\She holds her left wrist out between us. A thin gold chain hangs open across the bone, the tiny spring ring slipping from her thumb. #id:line.Day7.004
\“My hands are cold,” she says without looking up. “Do the clasp.” #id:line.Day7.005
\Two fingers take the gold link. Beneath my thumb, the skin on the inside of her wrist is warm, not cold at all; the pulse beats fast and shallow just beneath the tendon before she has even stepped back onto the floor. #id:line.Day7.006
\The spring ring clicks shut. #id:line.Day7.007
\Instead of letting go, my thumb passes once over the closed gold clasp and rests in the hollow of her wrist. #id:line.Day7.008
\Lavinia does not draw her arm back. #id:line.Day7.009
\“You're taking a long time over one catch,” she says softly. #id:line.Day7.010
\“It's small.” #id:line.Day7.011
\“Your hands aren't shaking.” #id:line.Day7.012
\“Yours aren't cold.” #id:line.Day7.013
\Her fingers curl once around the back of my wrist—holding my thumb against her pulse for the length of a breath—before she lets her arm fall to her side. #id:line.Day7.014
\On the dressing counter beside the rosin box lies a folded white card in Sylvia's neat, left-handed diagonal crease\: #id:line.Day7.015
\Quiet rest room reserved downstairs. Key at the desk if you want to leave by the lower gate. #id:line.Day7.016
\Lavinia sees where my gaze has stopped. #id:line.Day7.017
\“She left that before the first piece,” Lavinia says. “And the visitors in the front row asked for the second variation.” #id:line.Day7.018
\“The programme only promised one.” #id:line.Day7.019
\“I told the rehearsal director I'd do the second.” #id:line.Day7.020
\“With your right heel off the floor?” #id:line.Day7.021
\“It won't take long.” #id:line.Day7.022
\She bends toward the rosin box. Before her fingers reach the wood, her right knee gives a small, silent tremor under the skirt; her palm catches the edge of the counter and stays there a beat longer than the reach requires. #id:line.Day7.023
\“Don't say a word,” she says. #id:line.Day7.024
\“Nothing has been said.” #id:line.Day7.025
\She straightens and looks toward the rear exit glass, where rain is streaking the courtyard light. #id:line.Day7.026
\“After the second piece,” she says, her English flat and clear, “I'm taking the lower gate by myself. Not the bar. Not the flat. I want one evening where nobody is writing my schedule.” #id:line.Day7.027
\Cross the second piece off the stage-door board while her back is turned. #id:line.Day7.028
label Day7.thought
return

label Day7.clipboard.yes
\While Lavinia adjusts the cardigan over her shoulders, two silent steps reach the clipboard hanging beside the inner studio door. #id:line.Day7.clipboard.yes.001
\In the Second Variation / Floor Q&A row, one clean stroke of the pen crosses out Second Variation and writes beside it\: #id:line.Day7.clipboard.yes.002
\Session concluded. Clear hall. #id:line.Day7.clipboard.yes.003
\Behind her back, the pen cap clicks shut. #id:line.Day7.clipboard.yes.004
\“What did you just write?” Lavinia asks, turning around. #id:line.Day7.clipboard.yes.005
\“The visitor count exceeded the room limit. They have to clear the studio after the interlude.” #id:line.Day7.clipboard.yes.006
\“There are twenty people out there.” #id:line.Day7.clipboard.yes.007
\“Twenty-four. The fire notice says twenty.” #id:line.Day7.clipboard.yes.008
label Day7.clipboard.yes.next
return

label Day7.crossed.impulse
\Through the double acoustic panel, applause rises and thins as the pianist finishes the interlude. In a moment the stage manager will check the door. #id:line.Day7.crossed.impulse.001
\Lavinia takes one step toward the brass push-bar. Her right heel touches the floorboards, hesitates for a fraction of a second, and takes her weight anyway. #id:line.Day7.crossed.impulse.002
\“Move, Priscilla,” she says. “I'm doing the second piece, and then I'm going home alone.” #id:line.Day7.crossed.impulse.003
\Hold the door shut and give her the truth she's been trying to tear out of me all week. #id:line.Day7.crossed.impulse.004
label Day7.crossed.impulse.playable
label Day7.crossed.impulse.end
return

label Day7.crossed.D
\Before her fingertips touch the brass push-bar, my right hand closes around her left wrist—directly over the thin gold bracelet clasp—and draws her arm down from the door. My left palm plants flat against the heavy acoustic panel beside her cheek, holding the door shut with my weight. #id:line.Day7.crossed.D.001
\Lavinia's breath catches hard. #id:line.Day7.crossed.D.002
\“Let go.” #id:line.Day7.crossed.D.003
\“No.” #id:line.Day7.crossed.D.004
\“Tell me right now that you sent that form for my leg,” she whispers fiercely against my mouth, her free hand twisting into the wool at my lapel. “Go on. Use your committee voice one more time.” #id:line.Day7.crossed.D.005
\“It wasn't for your leg,” I whisper against her lips, every trace of polish gone, my voice softer than in the library and completely still. “You could have danced on it for another month. I altered the form because I couldn't bear the thought of someone else watching you in another city while I stayed here.” #id:line.Day7.crossed.D.006
\Against the heavy wood, Lavinia's shoulders go rigid; her eyes widen an inch from mine. #id:line.Day7.crossed.D.007
label Day7.crossed.D.echo
return

label Day7.crossed.D.recording
\“That's all?” she breathes. #id:line.Day7.crossed.D.recording.001
\“No,” I whisper, sliding my right hand from her gold bracelet to lace tightly between her fingers and pressing our joined hands flat against the door panel beside her hip. “When you asked for an answer on Monday, I recorded Sylvia reading a line out of her novel and sent it to your phone so you wouldn't hear how badly I wanted you back. And two minutes ago I crossed your second piece off the stage manager's board behind your back.” #id:line.Day7.crossed.D.recording.002
\Through the thick soundproof panel two inches behind her head, the stage manager's voice announces to the crowd that the performance has concluded. Chairs scrape back; twenty-four pairs of footsteps file out into the corridor and fade away. #id:line.Day7.crossed.D.recording.003
\The second piece is gone. #id:line.Day7.crossed.D.recording.004
\Lavinia does not call out through the door, and she does not let go of my lapel. Instead, her free hand slides up from my collar into the hair at the back of my neck, her fingers locking hard at my nape as she pulls my mouth down to hers—her breath hot, trembling, and electric against my lips. #id:line.Day7.crossed.D.recording.005
\“Ești un monstru,” she breathes against my mouth—and then in English, her forehead dropping hard against my collarbone\: “You're completely monstrous.” #id:line.Day7.crossed.D.recording.006
label Day7.crossed.D.recording.thought
return

label Day7.crossed.D.no_recording
\“That's all?” she breathes. #id:line.Day7.crossed.D.no_recording.001
\“No,” I whisper, sliding my right hand from her gold bracelet to lace tightly between her fingers and pressing our joined hands flat against the door panel beside her hip. “Two minutes ago I crossed your second piece off the stage manager's board behind your back.” #id:line.Day7.crossed.D.no_recording.002
\Through the thick soundproof panel two inches behind her head, the stage manager's voice announces to the crowd that the performance has concluded. Chairs scrape back; twenty-four pairs of footsteps file out into the corridor and fade away. #id:line.Day7.crossed.D.no_recording.003
\The second piece is gone. #id:line.Day7.crossed.D.no_recording.004
\Lavinia does not call out through the door, and she does not let go of my lapel. Instead, her free hand slides up from my collar into the hair at the back of my neck, her fingers locking hard at my nape as she pulls my mouth down to hers—her breath hot, trembling, and electric against my lips. #id:line.Day7.crossed.D.no_recording.005
\“Ești un monstru,” she breathes against my mouth—and then in English, her forehead dropping hard against my collarbone\: “You're completely monstrous.” #id:line.Day7.crossed.D.no_recording.006
label Day7.crossed.D.no_recording.thought
return

label Day7.crossed.D.board
\After I kneel to tie her street shoes and draw her coat over her shoulders, I pause for half a second beside the inner door where my black line strikes through Second Variation on the hanging clipboard. #id:line.Day7.crossed.D.board.001
\Lavinia looks at my handwriting on the board, then up at my mouth, her fingers tightening around mine as we turn toward the rear courtyard door. #id:line.Day7.crossed.D.board.002
label Day7.crossed.D.board.next
return

label Day7.clipboard.default
\The clipboard stays untouched on the hook; two steps place my shoulder directly between Lavinia and the inner studio door. #id:line.Day7.clipboard.default.001
\“The stage manager already moved the next slot to the main hall,” I say. #id:line.Day7.clipboard.default.002
\“He didn't tell me.” #id:line.Day7.clipboard.default.003
\“He asked me to tell you.” #id:line.Day7.clipboard.default.004
\Lavinia looks down at my empty hands, then up at my mouth. #id:line.Day7.clipboard.default.005
\“You're lying.” #id:line.Day7.clipboard.default.006
\“Go out and ask him. You'll have to walk across the floor on that right leg in front of twenty-four people to do it.” #id:line.Day7.clipboard.default.007
label Day7.clipboard.default.next
return

