label Day7.A
\“If you go back out on that floor,” I say quietly, “I'll bring a chair onto the stage for you in the middle of the piece.” #id:line.Day7.A.001
\Lavinia's hand stops an inch from the brass push-bar. #id:line.Day7.A.002
\“You wouldn't dare.” #id:line.Day7.A.003
\“The form is on the counter. It only takes one signature.” #id:line.Day7.A.004
\Lavinia turns slowly. Her chest rises fast; her eyes are bright and furious in the dressing-room light. #id:line.Day7.A.005
\“You really can't leave a single thing alone, can you?” #id:line.Day7.A.006
\“Not when it's you.” #id:line.Day7.A.007
\Beyond the wall, the stage manager announces that the session has concluded. Chairs scrape back; footsteps move toward the main hall. Lavinia lowers her hand from the brass bar and reaches for her street coat. #id:line.Day7.A.008
label Day7.A.next
return

label Day7.B
\My hand lifts toward her sleeve, then stops in the six inches of air between us without touching the wool. #id:line.Day7.B.001
\“Go on, then. Push the door open.” #id:line.Day7.B.002
\Lavinia looks at my stopped hand, then at the heavy acoustic door. #id:line.Day7.B.003
\To depress the bar and swing the soundproof panel outward, she has to plant her right foot flat and drive her weight through the ankle. She shifts her stance once—and stops. #id:line.Day7.B.004
\In the narrow wire-glass strip of the door, her reflection watches mine. #id:line.Day7.B.005
\Through the wall, the stage manager thanks the visitors for attending and directs them toward the foyer. The window for the second piece closes while neither of us moves. #id:line.Day7.B.006
\Lavinia lets out a short, quiet breath that sounds almost like a laugh. #id:line.Day7.B.007
\“You knew I'd wait a second too long,” she says, and reaches for her coat. #id:line.Day7.B.008
label Day7.B.next
return

label Day7.C
\“Don't open it,” I say quietly. “It wasn't only because of your leg.” #id:line.Day7.C.001
\Lavinia's fingers stop on the brass push-bar. She does not turn around right away. #id:line.Day7.C.002
\“What did you say?” #id:line.Day7.C.003
\“Before you went away. I told myself it was for your leg, and I told the department it was for your leg. But I sent the amendment without showing you because I knew that if you read it first, you would still get on the plane and leave me behind.” #id:line.Day7.C.004
\Slowly, Lavinia turns her back to the acoustic door. Through the thick wood behind her shoulder, the stage manager announces that the session has ended. Neither of us looks toward the hall. #id:line.Day7.C.005
\“You made me wait four months to hear you say that without an excuse,” she whispers. #id:line.Day7.C.006
\“I'm saying it now. Sit down and let me take your ribbons off.” #id:line.Day7.C.007
\She sinks onto the wooden bench and reaches down with unsteady fingers; I kneel on the floorboards in front of her, untie the pale satin from her right ankle, and slip her street shoe onto her foot before we walk out to the rear door. #id:line.Day7.C.008
label Day7.C.next
return

label Day7.D.default
\“Sit down,” I say gently, smoothing the hair back from her temple as if nothing had happened at the door. “Put your street shoes on before your ankle stiffens.” #id:line.Day7.D.default.001
\Lavinia sinks onto the wooden bench, her chest still rising fast, her eyes never leaving my face as I kneel at her feet, untie the rehearsal ribbons from her ankles, and slip both of her street shoes on for her. Before we step toward the rear passage, my hand lifts Sylvia's white lower-gate card from the counter and drops it into the bin beside the rosin box. #id:line.Day7.D.default.002
label Day7.D.default.next
return

label Day7.D.pocket
\While I button her street coat up to her chin, I take Sylvia's white lower-gate card from the counter, fold it once, and slip it into Lavinia's outer pocket before sliding my own hand in after it. #id:line.Day7.D.pocket.001
\“Keep the souvenir if you like,” I murmur against her ear. “We're taking the hill path.” #id:line.Day7.D.pocket.002
\Lavinia laughs once—breathless and dark—before I kneel to slip her street shoes onto her feet. #id:line.Day7.D.pocket.003
label Day7.D.pocket.next
return

label Day7.E
\“Don't open it,” I say quietly. “It wasn't only because of your leg.” #id:line.Day7.E.001
\Lavinia's fingers stop on the brass push-bar. She does not turn around right away. #id:line.Day7.E.002
\“What did you say?” #id:line.Day7.E.003
\“Before you went away. I told myself it was for your leg, and I told the department it was for your leg. But I sent the amendment without showing you because I knew that if you read it first, you would still get on the plane and leave me behind.” #id:line.Day7.E.004
\Slowly, Lavinia turns her back to the acoustic door. Through the thick wood behind her shoulder, the stage manager announces that the session has ended. Neither of us looks toward the hall. #id:line.Day7.E.005
\“You made me wait four months to hear you say that without an excuse,” she whispers. #id:line.Day7.E.006
\“I'm saying it now. Sit down and let me take your ribbons off.” #id:line.Day7.E.007
\She sinks onto the wooden bench and reaches down with unsteady fingers; I kneel on the floorboards in front of her, untie the pale satin from her right ankle, and slip her street shoe onto her foot before we walk out to the rear door. #id:line.Day7.E.008
\The stage manager knocked three minutes early. #id:line.Day7.E.009
\And through the frosted glass of the rear passage, the downstairs welfare desk—where Sylvia was scheduled to sit with the lower-gate key—is already dark, its chair folded against the wall, leaving only one unlocked exit to the courtyard. #id:line.Day7.E.010
\You. #id:line.Day7.E.011
\Seven days of mistimed clocks, rain showers, and reversed door cards, all narrowing like a corridor in a film to this bench. #id:line.Day7.E.012
\You wanted to see what remains when every other door is locked. #id:line.Day7.E.013
\Look closely, then. #id:line.Day7.E.014
label Day7.E.next
return

label ending.alone.normal
label ending.alone.normal.start
\At the rear doors of the building, the evening rain falls straight down through the courtyard lamp light. #id:line.ending.alone.normal.001
\The long black umbrella stands in the rack beside the threshold. #id:line.ending.alone.normal.002
\My right hand takes the curved wooden handle; my shoulder pushes the heavy door outward, and my thumb presses the catch. #id:line.ending.alone.normal.003
\The black canopy opens with a soft thrum against the wet air. #id:line.ending.alone.normal.004
\On the stone threshold, Lavinia buttons her coat to the chin. The heavy canvas bag hangs from her right shoulder. #id:line.ending.alone.normal.005
\Without asking, my left hand slips the canvas strap off her shoulder and settles it over my own arm. #id:line.ending.alone.normal.006
\Then I extend the umbrella over the top step—the wooden handle held in my right hand, tilting the canopy three inches toward my own side. #id:line.ending.alone.normal.007
\On the wet stone, the dry circle is barely wide enough for two pairs of shoes. #id:line.ending.alone.normal.008
\Lavinia looks at the rain falling past the black rim, then at the narrow dry space flush against my dry coat. #id:line.ending.alone.normal.009
\“That isn't very wide,” she says. #id:line.ending.alone.normal.010
\“It's the same umbrella as Tuesday.” #id:line.ending.alone.normal.011
\“You're holding it differently.” #id:line.ending.alone.normal.012
\“Come here.” #id:line.ending.alone.normal.013
\For a second she stands under the stone lintel. #id:line.ending.alone.normal.014
\Then she steps out under the canopy—close enough that her left arm presses the full length of my coat from shoulder to hip, her right foot taking the first step down with no weight on the turn. #id:line.ending.alone.normal.015
\At the bottom of the walk, where the hillside path divides toward the station and the flats, she does not look toward the station. #id:line.ending.alone.normal.016
\Her left hand slips inside the pocket of my coat, where five fingers are already waiting to close around hers. #id:line.ending.alone.normal.017
\“What time are you coming tomorrow?” she asks. #id:line.ending.alone.normal.018
\“Whenever you wake up.” #id:line.ending.alone.normal.019
\“That isn't a time.” #id:line.ending.alone.normal.020
\“Before then.” #id:line.ending.alone.normal.021
\Lavinia laughs once under her breath, rests her temple against my shoulder beneath the black canopy, and lets the turn take us up the hill. #id:line.ending.alone.normal.022
label ending.alone.normal.finish
return

